## 4. Statistical Significance of Base 2

### 4.1 The test

For each base b, we compute the crossover ratio `r_n` of `m_f(r) = kL/b^n` for n = 0..3, and find the minimum error over all particles. The base whose errors are smallest is statistically special.

### 4.2 The results

| Base | Mean error (n = 0..3) |
|---|---|
| **2** | **0.269%** |
| 1.4 | 0.662% |
| 3/2 | 0.672% |
| 4/3 | 0.685% |
| √2 | 0.732% |
| 1.1 | 0.775% |
| 1.6 | 0.845% |
| 5/4 | 0.971% |
| φ | 1.029% |
| 1.2 | 1.068% |
| 1.3 | 1.166% |
| 1.7 | 1.563% |
| 3 | 3.551% |
